<header><div class="eyebrow">Controlled fixture · source-grounded explanation</div><h1>One save in progress.<br>A button that reflects it.</h1><p class="lead">The helper now disables the Save button before calling persistence and enables it again when the attempt settles. A successful attempt still changes the label to “Saved”; a rejected attempt still rejects.</p><p>Only <code>editor.js:1</code> changes. The likely purpose is to prevent repeat user activation while a save is pending, inferred from the existing test named “prevent duplicate save.” No design, issue, or acceptance spec was supplied.</p></header>
<aside class="note"><strong>What is evidence here?</strong> This repository and its report are labeled controlled test data. No product tests or application were run for this explanation. The report’s image is explicitly identified as an unrelated GateReeve desktop capture and is excluded from the behavior figures below.</aside>
<section aria-labelledby="behavior"><h2 id="behavior">Follow the same save attempt</h2><p>The helper receives a button object and a <code>persist</code> callback. The callback represents the asynchronous write; its storage destination and callers are outside the supplied source. These diagrams assume an initially enabled button labeled “Save.”</p>
